Um, so great question. A lot of times that we talk about is first doing a forecast where we're

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:34.400
just trying to get it as mathematically close as possible. Like this is just our estimation.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.760
And then sometimes, you know, depending on what we're doing, we can also attach confidence

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:43.359
intervals to that. And then completely situationally dependent, we may say, okay,

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:48.880
this is our prediction, but we are going to, you know, bias it one way or another because of the

00:21:49.439 --> 00:21:55.839
downsides of certain things, right. And that all comes from really understanding the business

00:21:55.839 --> 00:21:59.920
question of what are the negative implications of getting it wrong in either direction.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:04.640
You know, sometimes we try to quantify that with, you know, especially like dollars for a comparison,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:08.640
because you can try to translate a lot of things to dollars format and say,

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:12.479
this is our negative implication on either side, and then say, this is what we're going to bias it.

00:22:12.479 --> 00:22:19.760
But we usually first start with, you know, what is our really best prediction. And I'll say there's

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:27.439
been times where different teams have different motivations for how they want forecasts to look,

00:22:27.439 --> 00:22:32.800
right, and what ways they kind of want it biased. And so, like, for example, I've seen times where

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:37.280
maybe our sales team wants to give our sales representative something that they can have a

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:41.680
good chance of beating, right, you don't want to, like, really make it too harsh of R, you kind of

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:46.319
want to keep positive momentum going. But if they're consistently beating it, and then you're

00:22:46.319 --> 00:22:50.160
using that, it's no longer really a valuable forecast for, like, operations perspective,

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:56.000
we're like, well, give us the true one, right. So I try to think about it both ways. And ideally,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:01.359
we try to forecast with confidence intervals when business is able to accept those kinds of nuance

00:23:01.359 --> 00:23:08.319
and the methodologies we're using work for it. And then sometimes we kind of have different

00:23:08.319 --> 00:23:15.599
